The function must compute the smallest axis-aligned rectangle enclosing a non-empty list of two-dimensional points. Such a rectangle has its sides parallel to the x and y axes, so its leftmost point is the smallest x coordinate in the list, its rightmost point is the largest, and the same applies to the y coordinates, with the smallest at the bottom and the largest at the top.

To find those four values, I walk through the list once. A careful initialisation is needed: the smallest x begins at plus infinity, the smallest y at plus infinity, and the largest x and largest y at minus infinity. With every point I take its x and y into account. When the list ends, the smallest of each coordinate is the lower bound on the respective axis, and the largest is the upper bound.

Because the first argument guarantees a list with at least one point, each of the four results is defined. A point with a single occurrence has the same smallest and largest value on both its axes, so the rectangle collapses into that point, as required, and none of the mins can finish at plus infinity.

The function returns these four values as a tuple in the order lower x, lower y, upper x, upper y, which is what the task asks for.

Verifying it against example calls on the three given inputs shows a few extra points come out correctly as well, and the method does not depend on any particular points.

