The function must decide whether one axis-aligned rectangle lies completely within another, reporting True even when their boundaries touch and False otherwise. Each rectangle is supplied as a tuple of (x1, y1, x2, y2), with x1 no larger than x2 and y1 no larger than y2, which means it spans the region from its lower left to its upper right.

To answer this, the inner rectangle describes a region with lower bound of min left at (inner.left) and lower bound of min width at (inner.bottom), and upper bound of max left at (inner.right) and upper bound of max width at (inner.top). A rectangle lies entirely within another only if, from every direction, the inner region does not pass its boundary. From the left, the outer rectangle's left boundary cannot be larger than the inner's; from the right, the inner's right boundary cannot be larger than the outer's; from below, the outer's bottom cannot be larger than the inner's; from above, the inner's top cannot be larger than the outer's. Equality on any edge is allowed since the specification says boundaries may touch. If all four of these hold, the outer contains the inner and the result is True; if not, it is False.

Because the rectangles are given with their corners in order, the outer rectangle's left coordinate is never after its right one, and the same holds for the widths, so no ordering of left and right would throw an error.

To keep the code idiomatic I wrote two small helper functions for the minima and maxima of each pair of coordinates, but they are only a simplification; the condition itself is a comparison.

The rectangles in the asserts are closed, so the boundaries are included, and each case agrees with the expected answer.

