A grid is non-empty when it has at least one row, and a row is non-empty when it has at least one column. The specification says at least one row exists, so an empty grid can be ignored; the empty-row guard is there only for economy.

Since every row has the same count of columns, the number of columns is just the length of the first row, and there is no need to check later rows. For each position from the first up to that count, I collect all the numbers stored there across the rows and sum them. The Python standard sums are traceable because adding an empty sequence gives zero. Rows shorter than the expected count can only appear in a grid that violates the stated condition, so they need no guard.

For the example with rows one-two and three-four, the first-position entries are 1 and 3, which sum to 4, and the second-position entries are 2 and 4, which sum to 6, so the expected column row appears. The single row of three fives gives three fives back, and the two rows of zeroes and ones produces 1 for each column. Returning an empty list when the grid is empty agrees with the requirement, although that case is never reached under the stated condition.

A final sum per column in position order is returned.

$$
