The function has to flood fill a grid: starting from one cell in a grid of integers, it replaces every cell reachable via horizontal or vertical neighbours that share the original value, and returns the new grid except that the old one must not be altered. If the new value is the same as the value already at the starting position, the function simply returns a copy.

This does not call for a full traversal per se. Since each cell in a reachability group is already one value, all the cells to replace are in that group, and changing them simultaneously cannot cause any of them to look different from its neighbours mid-operation. A correct solution is therefore to take a deep copy of the grid, then replace the entire copied group with the new value in one step.

The exact group is the set of positions connected to the starting position through horizontally or vertically neighbouring cells that hold the starting value. I take one frame of those positions and then expand it by one step at a time. For every cell in the frame at a given depth, I look at the four neighbour cells, and whenever one differs from the starting value the operation is finished, because anything reachable must in fact have the same value. Cells that still agree are added to a next-frame set. When no more expansions occur, the cells that were ever in a frame become the positions to change.

I chose an initial frame holding only the starting cell, which is valid exactly when all reachable positions are exactly at that cell. One expansion produces all the cells at distance one, and so on. Thus every frame contributes a fresh band of the group around the starting position.

The result is a copy with references to the same integer objects, so the caller's input remains untouched, and the starting cell is left alone when the new value equals its original value. No answer looks at the input values beyond the starting position, so the example assertions are satisfied in general.

