The input is a specification of numbers and intervals written as comma-separated tokens: a minus separates the two ends of an interval, and anything else is a single number. The output should be the sorted list of all non-negative integers covered, with no repeats.

Because the code is indexed by token rather than by value, the most efficient approach is to process each token in turn. A plain number is added directly to a set. An interval, which has exactly one minus, is split at that point, the two ends converted to integers, and each integer in the range is added to the set. That naturally handles overlapping intervals: inspecting the thirds of one interval and then the halves of a second, if it overlaps, inserts no value twice, and a set in Python preserves order only after it is turned back into a list, so we end up with one copy of each number. When the loop is done, the set is converted to an ordinary list and returned as the output.

